{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018KUu5Gy8b5BbxUn553FgkU","type":"tool_result","content":"# Brief — 2026-10-02\n\n## The use case (two sentences, from the scout)\nCompetitive sim racers (iRacing, Assetto Corsa, Le Mans Ultimate) want to share lap achievements as cards: sector times, deltas against personal best, visualized with bars or comparison stats. Communities at SimTelemetry.site and competitive leagues currently screenshot or manually create graphics; a deterministic lap card lets them share standardized achievements.\n\n## The template\n- id: @one-a-day/sports/lap-telemetry-card/v1\n- title: Sim Racing Lap Card\n- kind: image; canvas hint 1920x1080; must also work at 1080x1920 (portrait) and 1080x1080 (square)\n- duration: still\n\n## Layout (regions, ratios, what goes where; a small ASCII sketch is welcome)\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Track Name · Lap Time · Date       │ ← Header (top 20%): track, lap time, delta vs PB\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                     │\n│  Sector 1:  2:30.123  (PB 2:29.456) │\n│    Delta: -0.667s (green bar)       │\n│                                     │ ← Sectors (middle 60%):\n│  Sector 2:  2:45.234  (PB 2:44.123) │    3 rows, time + PB + delta bar\n│    Delta: -1.111s (green bar)       │\n│                                     │\n│  Sector 3:  2:29.766  (PB 2:29.123) │\n│    Delta: +0.643s (red bar)         │\n│                                     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Driver · Top Speed: 285 km/h       │ ← Footer (bottom 20%): driver, top speed\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## Layout contract (the invariants the build sweeps: text fits, bands, presence)\n- **Text fits**: track name up to 40 chars, lap time format M:SS.sss or SS.sss fits in header; sector labels and times fit in sector rows; all text checked at 0.94x cell width\n- **Header band**: occupies top 20% of canvas, contains track name, lap time, and overall delta\n- **Sector rows**: middle 60%, three equal-height rows for sectors 1, 2, 3; each shows time, PB time, delta, and a visual bar\n- **Footer band**: bottom 20%, driver name left-aligned, top speed right-aligned\n- **Presence**: every render shows track, lap time, three sectors with times and deltas, footer always present\n- **Visual delta bars**: proportional length, green for faster (negative delta), red for slower (positive delta); bars never exceed sector cell width\n\n## Props (name · type · default · what it changes · required?)\n1. trackName · string · \"Nürburgring\" · The circuit name · yes\n2. lapTime · string · \"7:45.123\" · Lap time in M:SS.sss format · yes\n3. pbTime · string · \"7:42.456\" · Personal best lap time · yes\n4. sector1Time · string · \"2:30.123\" · Sector 1 lap time · yes\n5. sector2Time · string · \"2:45.234\" · Sector 2 lap time · yes\n6. sector3Time · string · \"2:29.766\" · Sector 3 lap time · yes\n7. sector1Pb · string · \"2:29.456\" · Sector 1 personal best · yes\n8. sector2Pb · string · \"2:44.123\" · Sector 2 personal best · yes\n9. sector3Pb · string · \"2:29.123\" · Sector 3 personal best · yes\n10. driverId · string · \"Driver\" · Driver name or callsign · no\n11. topSpeed · string · \"285\" · Peak speed in km/h this lap · no\n12. date · string · (today) · Date of the lap attempt · no\n\n## Beats (video only)\nN/A — still image.\n\n## Defaults must show\nA realistic race-day lap with all three sectors faster than PB (negative deltas, green bars), showing a strong improvement run. Sector 1 and 2 highlight the biggest gains; sector 3 is slightly slower, showing the typical late-lap tire wear. Driver name and peak speed complete the context.\n\n## Acceptance rubric (the critic scores against this)\n1. **Arithmetic correctness**: deltas calculate correctly from (current time - PB time); sign and magnitude match the visual bar direction; times parse and format in M:SS.sss with no rounding artifacts\n2. **Text fits at all canvases**: track name, lap time, sector labels, times, deltas, driver name all fit legibly at 1920x1080, 1280x720, 1080x1920, 1080x1080, 3840x2160, 640x360, 480x270 (0.94x cell width floor)\n3. **Visual clarity**: delta bars are immediately readable; green/red distinction is clear even at small canvases; proportional bar length is accurate (longer bar = larger delta)\n4. **Default render shows the use case**: all three sectors visible, deltas calculated and displayed, driver and speed present, layout balanced and not cramped\n5. **No silent clipping**: validation rejects times with bad format (non-numeric, wrong separator, out of bounds); SVG text is tagged and contracted for every label\n\n## Variants worth trying (up to 3, each ONE idea different: a layout, a motion, a palette — not a rewrite)\n1. **Compact row layout** (sectors side-by-side instead of stacked): three-column grid where each sector occupies one column, times and deltas inline; trades height for width, optimizes for 1080x1080 square social crops\n2. **Apex speed focus** (replace sector 3 with apex speeds): shows turn-by-turn apex speeds at corners 1–5 instead of sector 3 times, for drivers who want turn-level telemetry highlights\n3. **Dark mode / accent color** (driver customization): card background and bar colors based on a team/driver accent color prop, for league identity or creator branding\n10-scout.md\n20-brief.md\n30-build.md\n40-critique.md\n40-critique.r1.md\n50-ship.md\ncontext.md\nlogs\nrejected\nrun.json\nstate.json\ntrace.json\nvariants\n# Critique — 2026-10-02\n\n## Scores\n| variant | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | total | fatal? |\n|---------|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|----|--------|\n| a | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 14/18 | YES |\n| b | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 18/18 | NO |\n\n## What each variant gets right / wrong\n\n**Variant A (round 1):**\nRenders cleanly at all canvases (landscape, portrait, square) with no degradation or error mosaics. Text fits legibly at all sizes. Layout ratios hold across aspects. **But:** the brief's core requirement — \"proportional delta bar (green for faster, red for slower, length proportional to the magnitude)\" — is missing entirely. The template renders only colored text deltas (+0.667s in red text, +1.111s in red text), not visual bar elements. The why-tutorial claims \"proportional delta bar\" but the default render (tutorial-5) proves this false.\n\n**Variant B (round 1 revision):**\nRenders at all canvases with exit 0 and no degradation (landscape 1920x1080, portrait 1080x1920, square 1080x1080). Defaults show the use case immediately: track (Nurburgring GP), lap time (1:54.812), improvement run (three sectors, first two green bars faster than PB, third red bar slightly slower). Text fits legibly at portrait (1080x1920) and square (1080x1080) without clipping or collapse. Layout proportions hold: 20% header, 60% sectors (three equal rows), 20% footer. Props are sensible and few: track, lap time, PB, three sectors with times and PBs, driver, top speed; all typed, all optional with realistic defaults (sector times and PBs mathematically consistent: sum to lap). A sim racer would use this instead of manually creating graphics or screenshotting. Code is deterministic, uses ctx.target, all text tagged and constrained (maxPx, maxLines), bars scale proportionally and never exceed track width. The brief promised proportional bars and the variant delivers them. Why-tutorial matches implementation: problem page cites scout's sources (SimTelemetry.site, Hacker News link) and findings; solution page describes \"proportional bar that is green when faster or red when slower\"; default render (tutorial-5) shows all three bars present and proportional; phases timeline shown.\n\n## Decision: SHIP B\n\n## Revision 1: what the earlier verdict asked for, and whether it is there\n\nThe earlier verdict said: \"Core visual feature (proportional delta bars) promised in brief is missing; template renders only colored text deltas, not bar elements.\"\n\n**All defects addressed and verified in variant B stills:**\n\n1. **Proportional delta bars now present** — Each sector row displays a solid colored rectangle below the delta text, with width proportional to |delta| against the maximum |delta| on the card. Landscape still shows S1 (-0.408s) with longest green bar, S2 (-0.325s) with slightly shorter green bar, S3 (+0.125s) with shortest red bar. Portrait and square confirm bars are visible and ranked by magnitude. ✓\n\n2. **Time format fixed** — All times now display in M:SS.sss format without extra zero. Landscape and portrait stills show \"1:54.812\" (not \"7:45.123\"), \"S1: 35.104\" (not \"2:030.123\"), \"S2: 41.236\", \"S3: 38.472\". Validation rejects bad format. ✓\n\n3. **Delta signs fixed** — Minus sign now present for faster sectors. Landscape shows \"-0.408s\" (green), \"-0.325s\" (green), \"+0.125s\" (red). Portrait confirms same. Brief's own prop table was overridden with correct defaults: S1 35.104 vs 35.512 (-0.408), S2 41.236 vs 41.561 (-0.325), S3 38.472 vs 38.347 (+0.125); three sector times sum to lap time (114.812), three PBs sum to lap PB (115.420), three deltas sum to lap delta (-0.608). ✓\n\n4. **Input validation in place** — parseTimeToMs (lines 281–298) validates format and throws on bad input with the prop name (e.g., \"Invalid time format: … (M:SS.sss format expected)\"). ✓\n\n5. **Footer alignment correct** — Driver name \"Driver\" left-aligned (x: 0.06), top speed \"285 km/h\" right-aligned (x: 0.54). Visible in landscape, portrait, square stills. ✓\n\n6. **Documentation matches implementation** — Header comment (lines 24–32) describes bars as proportional visual feature. Registry entry (registry.ts:31) says \"proportional delta bars (green for faster, red for slower)\". WHY.solution (line 206) describes \"proportional bar that is green when faster or red when slower than PB.\" Tutorial-3 (solution page) correctly states \"proportional bar that is green when faster or red when slower.\" ✓\n\n**All operator notes verified:** Time format (point 1), delta signs (point 2), defaults arithmetic (point 3), input validation (point 4), test coverage (not visually testable but claimed in build log), bars (point 6), footer alignment (point 7), documentation (point 8).\n\n## If ship: what a human polish pass should look at first\n\n- The bars are solid rectangles with no texture or pattern; consider whether shading, hatching, or gradients would improve clarity at very small canvases (480x270, 640x360) where bars may be thin.\n- Footer alignment uses static px positions (0.06 for driver, 0.54 for speed); confirm these look balanced at ultra-wide and ultra-narrow canvases (e.g., 3840x2160, 320x180).\n- Sector row heights (yFrac bands) are equal; consider whether a \"driver's eye view\" variant might weight sectors differently (e.g., if one sector is known to be more critical).\n\n## If no ship: the one thing that would change the verdict\n\nN/A — variant B ships.\n{\n  \"$comment\": \"The daily run. Edited by humans only — the agent never touches pipeline/. Times are wall-clock caps per agent call; a phase is re-called until it marks itself done in journal/<date>/state.json or runs out of calls.\",\n  \"agent\": \"claude\",\n  \"branch\": \"main\",\n  \"remote\": \"origin\",\n  \"dayTimeoutMin\": 270,\n  \"variantsMax\": 3,\n  \"limits\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"The account's session limit, as the agent CLI reports it (Claude Code streams the five-hour and seven-day windows on every call; Codex reports nothing, so a Codex day runs as before). A call the limit rejects does not count against the phase: the runner keeps the tree as journal/<date>/limited/tree.patch, waits for the window - never longer than waitMaxMin, which a five-hour window always fits and a seven-day one never does - and calls again, at most waitsPerDay times. waitAt: before a call, a five-hour window at or past this is waited out first, so the call is not cut halfway. sevenDayStopAt: preflight refuses the day when the seven-day window is past this, since a cut day could not wait for it. graceSec is added to every wait (the CLI's clock is not this machine's). Waits do not count against dayTimeoutMin, so the scheduler's own cap (ExecutionTimeLimit in schedule/one-a-day.task.xml) has to cover dayTimeoutMin + waitMaxMin.\",\n    \"waitAt\": 0.9,\n    \"waitMaxMin\": 300,\n    \"waitsPerDay\": 2,\n    \"graceSec\": 90,\n    \"sevenDayStopAt\": 0.95\n  },\n  \"phases\": [\n    { \"id\": \"scout\",    \"prompt\": \"10-scout.md\",    \"output\": \"10-scout.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 20, \"maxCalls\": 2 },\n    { \"id\": \"plan\",     \"prompt\": \"20-plan.md\",     \"output\": \"20-brief.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 15, \"maxCalls\": 2 },\n    { \"id\": \"build\",    \"prompt\": \"30-build.md\",    \"output\": \"30-build.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 45, \"maxCalls\": 3, \"revisePrompt\": \"35-revise.md\" },\n    { \"id\": \"critique\", \"prompt\": \"40-critique.md\", \"output\": \"40-critique.md\", \"timeoutMin\": 15, \"maxCalls\": 2, \"revisePrompt\": \"45-recritique.md\" },\n    { \"id\": \"ship\",     \"prompt\": \"50-ship.md\",     \"output\": \"50-ship.md\",     \"timeoutMin\": 30, \"maxCalls\": 2, \"onlyIf\": \"decision=ship\" }\n  ],\n  \"revise\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"The critic's NO SHIP is a review, not the end of the day (lib/revise.mjs). When the critic rejects work that exists - critique done, decision no-ship, at least one variant - the verdict moves aside as journal/<date>/40-critique.r<n>.md, build is called again with phases[].revisePrompt appended (answer the verdict, in the same id and folder, rendered into the next variant letter), and the critic judges again with its own revisePrompt. At most maxRounds rounds a run; 0 turns it off and the first verdict stands, as it did until day 013 (2026-10-02), which was closed 22 minutes into a 270-minute day with the fix named in the critique. Rounds live inside dayTimeoutMin; each build call still has its own cap and budget. A day that ends rejected keeps its tree as journal/<date>/rejected/tree.patch, and `node pipeline/run.mjs --date <date> --from revise` takes it up again (at least one round, same roster slot unless --roster or --model says otherwise).\",\n    \"maxRounds\": 2\n  },\n  \"packs\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"Preferred pack vocabulary. The plan phase picks an existing pack when one fits; a new pack needs one sentence of justification and at most one new pack lands per day.\",\n    \"vocabulary\": [\"social\", \"dev\", \"music\", \"education\", \"business\", \"science\", \"news\", \"personal\", \"ops\", \"community\", \"sports\", \"events\", \"health\", \"finance\"]\n  },\n  \"roster\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"Who runs today when the runner is called with --agent random (the scheduler is). One slot is drawn per day, weighted; the runner walks the draw order and takes the first CLI that is installed and logged in, so an agent that is not on this machine costs a log line, not the day. --roster <id> pins one slot by hand. avoidRepeat \\\"hard\\\" ranks yesterday agent behind every other one, so with two agents installed the CLI alternates and the weights choose WHICH slot of it runs; \\\"soft\\\" only damps the repeat (repeatPenalty) so the draw stays genuinely random; false forgets yesterday. entries[].adapter is merged over adapters.<agent> for that slot only. The spread is deliberate: the journal is more interesting when the same brief is attempted by a heavyweight one day and a cheap model the next, and a no-ship day from a small model is a real result, not a failure.\",\n    \"avoidRepeat\": \"hard\",\n    \"repeatPenalty\": { \"agent\": 0.3, \"slot\": 0.1 },\n    \"$models\": \"claude: aliases fable|opus|sonnet (+[1m] for the 1M-context variant) and haiku. codex: model slugs from the account, best to cheapest - gpt-6-astra (most capable), gpt-5.6-terra (balanced), gpt-5.6-sol (workhorse), gpt-5.6-luna (fast and affordable), gpt-5.5 (previous generation). Codex reasoning levels: low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultra.\",\n    \"$retired\": \"2026-09-30 (founder): every codex-* slot sits out (enabled: false) because OpenAI changed the usage limits on the Codex subscription ($20 Plus). Day 011 drained a fresh five-hour window in 13 minutes - scout, plan and 70 s of build at ultra - and the build failed 3/3 (journal/2026-09-30). Not a verdict on the models: the adapter, the pricing rows and the slots stay; flip enabled back to return one.\",\n    \"entries\": [\n      { \"id\": \"claude-opus-ultracode\", \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"opus[1m]\",     \"weight\": 4, \"adapter\": { \"ultracode\": true, \"effort\": \"xhigh\", \"budgetUsd\": 75 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-opus\",           \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"opus[1m]\",     \"weight\": 3, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 30 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-fable\",          \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"fable[1m]\",    \"weight\": 3, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 30 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-sonnet\",         \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet[1m]\",   \"weight\": 2, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 15 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-haiku\",          \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"haiku\",        \"weight\": 1, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"medium\", \"budgetUsd\": 8 } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-astra-ultra\",     \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-6-astra\",  \"weight\": 4, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"ultra\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-astra\",           \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-6-astra\",  \"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-terra\",           \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-sol\",             \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-sol\",  \"weight\": 2, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-luna\",            \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-luna\", \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"medium\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-5.5\",             \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",      \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"pricing\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"USD per MILLION tokens, by model-name substring (first match wins, so a specific name goes above its family), for the cost ESTIMATE on the why-tutorial's timeline page when the agent CLI reports no cost itself (Claude Code reports total_cost_usd; Codex does not). Edit to the list prices of the day and bump pricedAt - the card prints the date; a row with its own pricedAt was checked on that date, the rest on the table's. Claude rows: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing on 2026-09-26; cacheWrite is the 1-hour write (2x input), which is the one Claude Code sessions write. The gpt/codex rows were not re-checked (day 5's audit found gpt-5.6-sol at $4 / $0.40 cached / $20). Missing model = no estimate, tokens only.\",\n    \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-20\",\n    \"perMillion\": {\n      \"claude-opus-5-5\": { \"input\": 4, \"output\": 20, \"cacheRead\": 0.2, \"cacheWrite\": 8, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-fable-5-1\": { \"input\": 10, \"output\": 50, \"cacheRead\": 0.25, \"cacheWrite\": 20, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-fable\": { \"input\": 10, \"output\": 50, \"cacheRead\": 1, \"cacheWrite\": 20, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-opus\": { \"input\": 5, \"output\": 25, \"cacheRead\": 0.5, \"cacheWrite\": 10, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-sonnet-5\": { \"input\": 2, \"output\": 10, \"cacheRead\": 0.2, \"cacheWrite\": 4, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-sonnet\": { \"input\": 3, \"output\": 15, \"cacheRead\": 0.3, \"cacheWrite\": 6, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"910ebd97-ff9f-4cc5-acb0-6adbd2391433","uuid":"037fd3de-7edf-47bf-9c1a-d006111b9e16","timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:08:06.248Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Brief — 2026-10-02\n\n## The use case (two sentences, from the scout)\nCompetitive sim racers (iRacing, Assetto Corsa, Le Mans Ultimate) want to share lap achievements as cards: sector times, deltas against personal best, visualized with bars or comparison stats. Communities at SimTelemetry.site and competitive leagues currently screenshot or manually create graphics; a deterministic lap card lets them share standardized achievements.\n\n## The template\n- id: @one-a-day/sports/lap-telemetry-card/v1\n- title: Sim Racing Lap Card\n- kind: image; canvas hint 1920x1080; must also work at 1080x1920 (portrait) and 1080x1080 (square)\n- duration: still\n\n## Layout (regions, ratios, what goes where; a small ASCII sketch is welcome)\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Track Name · Lap Time · Date       │ ← Header (top 20%): track, lap time, delta vs PB\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                                     │\n│  Sector 1:  2:30.123  (PB 2:29.456) │\n│    Delta: -0.667s (green bar)       │\n│                                     │ ← Sectors (middle 60%):\n│  Sector 2:  2:45.234  (PB 2:44.123) │    3 rows, time + PB + delta bar\n│    Delta: -1.111s (green bar)       │\n│                                     │\n│  Sector 3:  2:29.766  (PB 2:29.123) │\n│    Delta: +0.643s (red bar)         │\n│                                     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Driver · Top Speed: 285 km/h       │ ← Footer (bottom 20%): driver, top speed\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## Layout contract (the invariants the build sweeps: text fits, bands, presence)\n- **Text fits**: track name up to 40 chars, lap time format M:SS.sss or SS.sss fits in header; sector labels and times fit in sector rows; all text checked at 0.94x cell width\n- **Header band**: occupies top 20% of canvas, contains track name, lap time, and overall delta\n- **Sector rows**: middle 60%, three equal-height rows for sectors 1, 2, 3; each shows time, PB time, delta, and a visual bar\n- **Footer band**: bottom 20%, driver name left-aligned, top speed right-aligned\n- **Presence**: every render shows track, lap time, three sectors with times and deltas, footer always present\n- **Visual delta bars**: proportional length, green for faster (negative delta), red for slower (positive delta); bars never exceed sector cell width\n\n## Props (name · type · default · what it changes · required?)\n1. trackName · string · \"Nürburgring\" · The circuit name · yes\n2. lapTime · string · \"7:45.123\" · Lap time in M:SS.sss format · yes\n3. pbTime · string · \"7:42.456\" · Personal best lap time · yes\n4. sector1Time · string · \"2:30.123\" · Sector 1 lap time · yes\n5. sector2Time · string · \"2:45.234\" · Sector 2 lap time · yes\n6. sector3Time · string · \"2:29.766\" · Sector 3 lap time · yes\n7. sector1Pb · string · \"2:29.456\" · Sector 1 personal best · yes\n8. sector2Pb · string · \"2:44.123\" · Sector 2 personal best · yes\n9. sector3Pb · string · \"2:29.123\" · Sector 3 personal best · yes\n10. driverId · string · \"Driver\" · Driver name or callsign · no\n11. topSpeed · string · \"285\" · Peak speed in km/h this lap · no\n12. date · string · (today) · Date of the lap attempt · no\n\n## Beats (video only)\nN/A — still image.\n\n## Defaults must show\nA realistic race-day lap with all three sectors faster than PB (negative deltas, green bars), showing a strong improvement run. Sector 1 and 2 highlight the biggest gains; sector 3 is slightly slower, showing the typical late-lap tire wear. Driver name and peak speed complete the context.\n\n## Acceptance rubric (the critic scores against this)\n1. **Arithmetic correctness**: deltas calculate correctly from (current time - PB time); sign and magnitude match the visual bar direction; times parse and format in M:SS.sss with no rounding artifacts\n2. **Text fits at all canvases**: track name, lap time, sector labels, times, deltas, driver name all fit legibly at 1920x1080, 1280x720, 1080x1920, 1080x1080, 3840x2160, 640x360, 480x270 (0.94x cell width floor)\n3. **Visual clarity**: delta bars are immediately readable; green/red distinction is clear even at small canvases; proportional bar length is accurate (longer bar = larger delta)\n4. **Default render shows the use case**: all three sectors visible, deltas calculated and displayed, driver and speed present, layout balanced and not cramped\n5. **No silent clipping**: validation rejects times with bad format (non-numeric, wrong separator, out of bounds); SVG text is tagged and contracted for every label\n\n## Variants worth trying (up to 3, each ONE idea different: a layout, a motion, a palette — not a rewrite)\n1. **Compact row layout** (sectors side-by-side instead of stacked): three-column grid where each sector occupies one column, times and deltas inline; trades height for width, optimizes for 1080x1080 square social crops\n2. **Apex speed focus** (replace sector 3 with apex speeds): shows turn-by-turn apex speeds at corners 1–5 instead of sector 3 times, for drivers who want turn-level telemetry highlights\n3. **Dark mode / accent color** (driver customization): card background and bar colors based on a team/driver accent color prop, for league identity or creator branding\n10-scout.md\n20-brief.md\n30-build.md\n40-critique.md\n40-critique.r1.md\n50-ship.md\ncontext.md\nlogs\nrejected\nrun.json\nstate.json\ntrace.json\nvariants\n# Critique — 2026-10-02\n\n## Scores\n| variant | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | total | fatal? |\n|---------|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|----|--------|\n| a | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 14/18 | YES |\n| b | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 18/18 | NO |\n\n## What each variant gets right / wrong\n\n**Variant A (round 1):**\nRenders cleanly at all canvases (landscape, portrait, square) with no degradation or error mosaics. Text fits legibly at all sizes. Layout ratios hold across aspects. **But:** the brief's core requirement — \"proportional delta bar (green for faster, red for slower, length proportional to the magnitude)\" — is missing entirely. The template renders only colored text deltas (+0.667s in red text, +1.111s in red text), not visual bar elements. The why-tutorial claims \"proportional delta bar\" but the default render (tutorial-5) proves this false.\n\n**Variant B (round 1 revision):**\nRenders at all canvases with exit 0 and no degradation (landscape 1920x1080, portrait 1080x1920, square 1080x1080). Defaults show the use case immediately: track (Nurburgring GP), lap time (1:54.812), improvement run (three sectors, first two green bars faster than PB, third red bar slightly slower). Text fits legibly at portrait (1080x1920) and square (1080x1080) without clipping or collapse. Layout proportions hold: 20% header, 60% sectors (three equal rows), 20% footer. Props are sensible and few: track, lap time, PB, three sectors with times and PBs, driver, top speed; all typed, all optional with realistic defaults (sector times and PBs mathematically consistent: sum to lap). A sim racer would use this instead of manually creating graphics or screenshotting. Code is deterministic, uses ctx.target, all text tagged and constrained (maxPx, maxLines), bars scale proportionally and never exceed track width. The brief promised proportional bars and the variant delivers them. Why-tutorial matches implementation: problem page cites scout's sources (SimTelemetry.site, Hacker News link) and findings; solution page describes \"proportional bar that is green when faster or red when slower\"; default render (tutorial-5) shows all three bars present and proportional; phases timeline shown.\n\n## Decision: SHIP B\n\n## Revision 1: what the earlier verdict asked for, and whether it is there\n\nThe earlier verdict said: \"Core visual feature (proportional delta bars) promised in brief is missing; template renders only colored text deltas, not bar elements.\"\n\n**All defects addressed and verified in variant B stills:**\n\n1. **Proportional delta bars now present** — Each sector row displays a solid colored rectangle below the delta text, with width proportional to |delta| against the maximum |delta| on the card. Landscape still shows S1 (-0.408s) with longest green bar, S2 (-0.325s) with slightly shorter green bar, S3 (+0.125s) with shortest red bar. Portrait and square confirm bars are visible and ranked by magnitude. ✓\n\n2. **Time format fixed** — All times now display in M:SS.sss format without extra zero. Landscape and portrait stills show \"1:54.812\" (not \"7:45.123\"), \"S1: 35.104\" (not \"2:030.123\"), \"S2: 41.236\", \"S3: 38.472\". Validation rejects bad format. ✓\n\n3. **Delta signs fixed** — Minus sign now present for faster sectors. Landscape shows \"-0.408s\" (green), \"-0.325s\" (green), \"+0.125s\" (red). Portrait confirms same. Brief's own prop table was overridden with correct defaults: S1 35.104 vs 35.512 (-0.408), S2 41.236 vs 41.561 (-0.325), S3 38.472 vs 38.347 (+0.125); three sector times sum to lap time (114.812), three PBs sum to lap PB (115.420), three deltas sum to lap delta (-0.608). ✓\n\n4. **Input validation in place** — parseTimeToMs (lines 281–298) validates format and throws on bad input with the prop name (e.g., \"Invalid time format: … (M:SS.sss format expected)\"). ✓\n\n5. **Footer alignment correct** — Driver name \"Driver\" left-aligned (x: 0.06), top speed \"285 km/h\" right-aligned (x: 0.54). Visible in landscape, portrait, square stills. ✓\n\n6. **Documentation matches implementation** — Header comment (lines 24–32) describes bars as proportional visual feature. Registry entry (registry.ts:31) says \"proportional delta bars (green for faster, red for slower)\". WHY.solution (line 206) describes \"proportional bar that is green when faster or red when slower than PB.\" Tutorial-3 (solution page) correctly states \"proportional bar that is green when faster or red when slower.\" ✓\n\n**All operator notes verified:** Time format (point 1), delta signs (point 2), defaults arithmetic (point 3), input validation (point 4), test coverage (not visually testable but claimed in build log), bars (point 6), footer alignment (point 7), documentation (point 8).\n\n## If ship: what a human polish pass should look at first\n\n- The bars are solid rectangles with no texture or pattern; consider whether shading, hatching, or gradients would improve clarity at very small canvases (480x270, 640x360) where bars may be thin.\n- Footer alignment uses static px positions (0.06 for driver, 0.54 for speed); confirm these look balanced at ultra-wide and ultra-narrow canvases (e.g., 3840x2160, 320x180).\n- Sector row heights (yFrac bands) are equal; consider whether a \"driver's eye view\" variant might weight sectors differently (e.g., if one sector is known to be more critical).\n\n## If no ship: the one thing that would change the verdict\n\nN/A — variant B ships.\n{\n  \"$comment\": \"The daily run. Edited by humans only — the agent never touches pipeline/. Times are wall-clock caps per agent call; a phase is re-called until it marks itself done in journal/<date>/state.json or runs out of calls.\",\n  \"agent\": \"claude\",\n  \"branch\": \"main\",\n  \"remote\": \"origin\",\n  \"dayTimeoutMin\": 270,\n  \"variantsMax\": 3,\n  \"limits\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"The account's session limit, as the agent CLI reports it (Claude Code streams the five-hour and seven-day windows on every call; Codex reports nothing, so a Codex day runs as before). A call the limit rejects does not count against the phase: the runner keeps the tree as journal/<date>/limited/tree.patch, waits for the window - never longer than waitMaxMin, which a five-hour window always fits and a seven-day one never does - and calls again, at most waitsPerDay times. waitAt: before a call, a five-hour window at or past this is waited out first, so the call is not cut halfway. sevenDayStopAt: preflight refuses the day when the seven-day window is past this, since a cut day could not wait for it. graceSec is added to every wait (the CLI's clock is not this machine's). Waits do not count against dayTimeoutMin, so the scheduler's own cap (ExecutionTimeLimit in schedule/one-a-day.task.xml) has to cover dayTimeoutMin + waitMaxMin.\",\n    \"waitAt\": 0.9,\n    \"waitMaxMin\": 300,\n    \"waitsPerDay\": 2,\n    \"graceSec\": 90,\n    \"sevenDayStopAt\": 0.95\n  },\n  \"phases\": [\n    { \"id\": \"scout\",    \"prompt\": \"10-scout.md\",    \"output\": \"10-scout.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 20, \"maxCalls\": 2 },\n    { \"id\": \"plan\",     \"prompt\": \"20-plan.md\",     \"output\": \"20-brief.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 15, \"maxCalls\": 2 },\n    { \"id\": \"build\",    \"prompt\": \"30-build.md\",    \"output\": \"30-build.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 45, \"maxCalls\": 3, \"revisePrompt\": \"35-revise.md\" },\n    { \"id\": \"critique\", \"prompt\": \"40-critique.md\", \"output\": \"40-critique.md\", \"timeoutMin\": 15, \"maxCalls\": 2, \"revisePrompt\": \"45-recritique.md\" },\n    { \"id\": \"ship\",     \"prompt\": \"50-ship.md\",     \"output\": \"50-ship.md\",     \"timeoutMin\": 30, \"maxCalls\": 2, \"onlyIf\": \"decision=ship\" }\n  ],\n  \"revise\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"The critic's NO SHIP is a review, not the end of the day (lib/revise.mjs). When the critic rejects work that exists - critique done, decision no-ship, at least one variant - the verdict moves aside as journal/<date>/40-critique.r<n>.md, build is called again with phases[].revisePrompt appended (answer the verdict, in the same id and folder, rendered into the next variant letter), and the critic judges again with its own revisePrompt. At most maxRounds rounds a run; 0 turns it off and the first verdict stands, as it did until day 013 (2026-10-02), which was closed 22 minutes into a 270-minute day with the fix named in the critique. Rounds live inside dayTimeoutMin; each build call still has its own cap and budget. A day that ends rejected keeps its tree as journal/<date>/rejected/tree.patch, and `node pipeline/run.mjs --date <date> --from revise` takes it up again (at least one round, same roster slot unless --roster or --model says otherwise).\",\n    \"maxRounds\": 2\n  },\n  \"packs\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"Preferred pack vocabulary. The plan phase picks an existing pack when one fits; a new pack needs one sentence of justification and at most one new pack lands per day.\",\n    \"vocabulary\": [\"social\", \"dev\", \"music\", \"education\", \"business\", \"science\", \"news\", \"personal\", \"ops\", \"community\", \"sports\", \"events\", \"health\", \"finance\"]\n  },\n  \"roster\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"Who runs today when the runner is called with --agent random (the scheduler is). One slot is drawn per day, weighted; the runner walks the draw order and takes the first CLI that is installed and logged in, so an agent that is not on this machine costs a log line, not the day. --roster <id> pins one slot by hand. avoidRepeat \\\"hard\\\" ranks yesterday agent behind every other one, so with two agents installed the CLI alternates and the weights choose WHICH slot of it runs; \\\"soft\\\" only damps the repeat (repeatPenalty) so the draw stays genuinely random; false forgets yesterday. entries[].adapter is merged over adapters.<agent> for that slot only. The spread is deliberate: the journal is more interesting when the same brief is attempted by a heavyweight one day and a cheap model the next, and a no-ship day from a small model is a real result, not a failure.\",\n    \"avoidRepeat\": \"hard\",\n    \"repeatPenalty\": { \"agent\": 0.3, \"slot\": 0.1 },\n    \"$models\": \"claude: aliases fable|opus|sonnet (+[1m] for the 1M-context variant) and haiku. codex: model slugs from the account, best to cheapest - gpt-6-astra (most capable), gpt-5.6-terra (balanced), gpt-5.6-sol (workhorse), gpt-5.6-luna (fast and affordable), gpt-5.5 (previous generation). Codex reasoning levels: low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultra.\",\n    \"$retired\": \"2026-09-30 (founder): every codex-* slot sits out (enabled: false) because OpenAI changed the usage limits on the Codex subscription ($20 Plus). Day 011 drained a fresh five-hour window in 13 minutes - scout, plan and 70 s of build at ultra - and the build failed 3/3 (journal/2026-09-30). Not a verdict on the models: the adapter, the pricing rows and the slots stay; flip enabled back to return one.\",\n    \"entries\": [\n      { \"id\": \"claude-opus-ultracode\", \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"opus[1m]\",     \"weight\": 4, \"adapter\": { \"ultracode\": true, \"effort\": \"xhigh\", \"budgetUsd\": 75 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-opus\",           \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"opus[1m]\",     \"weight\": 3, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 30 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-fable\",          \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"fable[1m]\",    \"weight\": 3, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 30 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-sonnet\",         \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet[1m]\",   \"weight\": 2, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 15 } },\n      { \"id\": \"claude-haiku\",          \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"haiku\",        \"weight\": 1, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"medium\", \"budgetUsd\": 8 } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-astra-ultra\",     \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-6-astra\",  \"weight\": 4, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"ultra\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-astra\",           \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-6-astra\",  \"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-terra\",           \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-sol\",             \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-sol\",  \"weight\": 2, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-luna\",            \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-luna\", \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"medium\" } },\n      { \"id\": \"codex-5.5\",             \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",      \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"pricing\": {\n    \"$comment\": \"USD per MILLION tokens, by model-name substring (first match wins, so a specific name goes above its family), for the cost ESTIMATE on the why-tutorial's timeline page when the agent CLI reports no cost itself (Claude Code reports total_cost_usd; Codex does not). Edit to the list prices of the day and bump pricedAt - the card prints the date; a row with its own pricedAt was checked on that date, the rest on the table's. Claude rows: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing on 2026-09-26; cacheWrite is the 1-hour write (2x input), which is the one Claude Code sessions write. The gpt/codex rows were not re-checked (day 5's audit found gpt-5.6-sol at $4 / $0.40 cached / $20). Missing model = no estimate, tokens only.\",\n    \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-20\",\n    \"perMillion\": {\n      \"claude-opus-5-5\": { \"input\": 4, \"output\": 20, \"cacheRead\": 0.2, \"cacheWrite\": 8, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-fable-5-1\": { \"input\": 10, \"output\": 50, \"cacheRead\": 0.25, \"cacheWrite\": 20, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-fable\": { \"input\": 10, \"output\": 50, \"cacheRead\": 1, \"cacheWrite\": 20, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-opus\": { \"input\": 5, \"output\": 25, \"cacheRead\": 0.5, \"cacheWrite\": 10, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-sonnet-5\": { \"input\": 2, \"output\": 10, \"cacheRead\": 0.2, \"cacheWrite\": 4, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },\n      \"claude-sonnet\": { \"input\": 3, \"output\": 15, \"cacheRead\": 0.3, \"cacheWrite\": 6, \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-26\" },","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
