{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffMrapCSCZoAgXwa2Uq8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plan phase is done. The brief is in `journal/2026-10-03/20-brief.md`, and `state.json` now has `pack`, `slug`, `title` and `plan: \"done\"`. `run.json` already had `model.selfDeclared: \"claude-opus-5-5\"`; I wrote it again in my first step and changed nothing else.\n\nThe ASCII check on the brief didn't run, because this shell's grep doesn't support `-P`. The headings use the em dashes the prompt's own template uses. I didn't check the rest of the text for other non-ASCII characters.\n\n**What gets built:** `@one-a-day/community/qsl-card/v1`, titled \"Ham Radio QSL Card\". It's a still image with a 1920x1080 hint, and it also has to render cleanly at the physical card's 11:7 size (1650x1050). `community` is a new pack from the preferred list: a QSL card is something hobby members trade with each other, so it doesn't fit `sports` or `events`.\n\n- **Layout:** four full-width bands under a thin red accent bar:\n  - a header where the station callsign is the biggest thing on the card, with grid, QTH and POTA ref beside it;\n  - a \"TO RADIO <call>\" strip;\n  - a six-cell QSO table (DATE, UTC, MHz, BAND, MODE, RST) that reflows by aspect: one row in landscape, 3x2 in square, 2x3 in portrait;\n  - a footer with the QSL message.\n- **Props:** 12 plus `debugLayout`, each named after its ADIF field so an operator can map a log record by eye. Dates like `20261003` render as `03 OCT 2026` and times like `1432` as `14:32`. BAND is worked out from FREQ using the ADIF band table, so the two can't disagree. Bad input fails fast and names the prop: an impossible date or time, a frequency outside the amateur bands, a bad grid square, or a callsign with a space.\n- **Layout contract:** every text gets its own fit label, and the test sweeps worst-case callsigns like `VP2V/G4ABC/P`. The callsign stays in the top half, all six table values must be present, the footer sits in the bottom band and the accent bar is full width. The test also checks that the callsign is at least twice the size of the table values. The callsign is never cut short with an ellipsis.\n- **Defaults:** N0CALL (the usual placeholder callsign in ham software) to W1AW (the ARRL station hams use as their stock example), on 14.074 FT8 with a report of -12. They are labelled as examples, not anyone's real log.\n- **Variants:**\n  - a: the stacked bands (baseline);\n  - b: a classic left station panel with the data on the right;\n  - c: the QSO table drawn as a ruled, pre-printed form.\n\n**Known weak spot:** there's no photo background in v1, because the repo ships no media. The card has to look right from colour, geometry and type alone, and the brief asks the tutorial and registry description to say so."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":498,"cache_read_input_tokens":62227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":498},"output_tokens":761,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"910ebd97-ff9f-4cc5-acb0-6adbd2391433","uuid":"d818fba1-c13e-468c-84c5-b8d434fe1eb7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:11:42.150Z","request_id":"req_011CffMraRsdVtRg6B54s95Q"}
